  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Acidic hydrogen is a hydrogen atom in an acid that dissociates to generate a positive ion.</p><p>After removal of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>ion, the negative charge is formed. The more stable the negative charge, the more will be the acidic strength.</p><p>Due to cross conjugation of negative charge, it is<br/>most stabilised anion.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0144_0859e087cc0a.png\" style=\"width: 400px; height: 379px;\"/></p><p>Hence, the answer is option D.</p>"
  }
